# CEO's OS — Security Review Rule

## Purpose

Security gate for authentication, authorization, organizationId scoping, multi-tenant isolation, secrets protection, safe logging, and auditability on enterprise state-changing actions.

This rule applies to every change that touches APIs, auth, persistence, exports, sharing, or sensitive data paths.

## Mandatory Security Gates

Before accepting or implementing a sensitive change, verify:

| Gate | Requirement |
|---|---|
| Endpoint protection | Route requires authenticated session/token unless explicitly public by design |
| Token/session | Validated server-side; not trust client-only flags |
| organizationId | Comes from backend/token/session; never chosen by frontend as authority |
| Frontend ownership | Frontend must not decide organization ownership or tenant scope |
| Role/permission | Enforced server-side on every mutating action |
| Viewer role | Viewer cannot create/update/delete enterprise business data |
| Mutations | POST/PATCH/DELETE protected with explicit permission checks |
| Input validation | Payload schema validated; reject malformed or oversized input |
| Cross-tenant | No query or mutation may return or modify another organization's data |
| Error responses | No secrets, stack traces with credentials, or internal paths in client production errors |
| Logs | No AUTH_SECRET, tokens, passwords, API keys, PII, or production DB dumps |
| Audit events | State-changing enterprise actions preserve actor, organizationId, action, timestamp, safe metadata |
| Environment files | Only `.env.example` for placeholders; never commit real `.env` values |

## Role / Permission Rule

Do not assume role names or permission strings from memory.

Verify the current implementation in:

- `backend/api/middlewares/auth.middleware.js`
- `src/app/providers/AuthProvider.jsx` (for UI gating only; backend remains authoritative)

If permission model is unclear, stop and escalate to human review.

Status: Assumed / Pending C.13 validation for complete role matrix per module endpoint.

## Multi-Tenant Rule

Any business read, write, update, or delete must be scoped server-side by `organizationId`.

Forbidden patterns:

- Accepting `organizationId` from client body as authoritative without server validation
- Using a global query without organization filter on tenant data tables
- Returning list results from all organizations to admin without explicit super-admin design and audit

## Auditability Rule

State-changing enterprise actions should preserve:

- actor (user id or system actor label)
- organizationId
- action type
- timestamp
- safe metadata (no secrets, no full payloads with PII unless required and approved)

Examples: create deal, approve governance decision, export report, acknowledge bridge signal, update PMI case.

Status: Assumed / Pending C.13 validation per module audit trail coverage.

## Secrets and Sensitive Data Guard

Never print, commit, generate, or expose:

- AUTH_SECRET
- session tokens
- passwords
- API keys
- private URLs with credentials
- production database contents
- customer emails or personal data in logs
- `.env` values

## Stop Conditions

Stop and report before changing code if:

- organizationId source is unclear
- permission model is unclear for the endpoint
- viewer role may mutate data
- endpoint may expose cross-tenant data
- secrets may leak in logs or responses
- production data may be copied into prompts or commits
- auth/router/middleware changes are required without explicit authorization in task scope
- migration may delete or rewrite tenant data without backup plan

## Human Review Escalation

Escalate when security impact is ambiguous:

- new public endpoint
- new export/share feature
- permission reduction or expansion
- cross-module auth change
- audit log format change affecting compliance narrative
